Q: How can a Chrome extension automatically initialize a Supabase database schema for users without requiring them to run SQL manually? A: <p>The best approach for your case is <strong>Option 2 — Supabase Edge Functions.</strong> Here is why and how to implement it properly.</p>
<p><strong>The flow:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><p>User enters their Supabase URL + anon key in your extension</p>
</li>
<li><p>Your extension calls an Edge Function in <strong>your own</strong> Supabase project (not the user's)</p>
</li>
<li><p>That Edge Function uses the user's <code>service_role</code> key temporarily to create the schema in their project</p>
</li>
<li><p>Edge Function deletes the key from memory after setup is done</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>But wait — this still requires the user's <code>service_role</code> key which you want to avoid. The cleanest real solution is:</p>
<p><strong>Use Supabase Management API:</strong></p>
<pre><code>// In your Chrome extension background script
async function initializeSchema(userSupabaseUrl, userAnonKey) {
  // Step 1: Check if schema exists
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from('issues')
    .select('id')
    .limit(1);

  if (error &amp;&amp; error.code === '42P01') {
    // Table doesn't exist — run setup
    await runMigration(userSupabaseUrl, userAnonKey);
  }
}

async function runMigration(url, key) {
  const sql = `
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS issues (
      id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
      github_issue_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
      title TEXT NOT NULL
    );
    ALTER TABLE issues ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
  `;

  // Use Supabase REST API to execute SQL
  await fetch(`${url}/rest/v1/rpc/exec_sql`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'apikey': key,
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${key}`,
      'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({ query: sql })
  });
}
</code></pre>
